+================================================================================
+ left_gate gsplat render — reproduction handoff
+ Target: a DIFFERENT machine with the OLD code (sagesplat / Splat-MOVER stack),
+ to reproduce the falsify render of configs/scenes/left_gate.yaml in a
+ standalone gsplat-1.5.3 script (no nerfstudio / no FiGS).
+
+ Extracted from the working falsify renderer. Numbers are exact (float32 repr).
+================================================================================
+
+SOURCE ENVIRONMENT (where these numbers came from)
+--------------------------------------------------
+- model class : sagesplat.sagesplat.SageSplatModel   (Splat-MOVER)
+- gsplat      : 0.1.13  — has NO gsplat.rendering.rasterization, so sagesplat
+                defines its OWN `rasterization(...)` shim (a gsplat-1.x-style API
+                reimplemented on top of 0.1.13). The kwargs it is called with are
+                exactly a gsplat-1.x rasterization() call, so on a machine with
+                real gsplat-1.5.3 you feed the SAME kwargs to gsplat.rasterization.
+- checkpoint  : data/gate_scenes_export/left_scene/mocap_outputs/sagesplat_mocap/
+                sagesplat/2026-05-11_153901/nerfstudio_models/step-000029999.ckpt
+- N gaussians : 6,087,883
+- gauss params (raw ckpt) shapes:
+    means        (N, 3)
+    scales       (N, 3)     # log-space
+    quats        (N, 4)     # UNNORMALIZED in ckpt
+    opacities    (N, 1)     # logits
+    features_dc  (N, 3)     # SH DC term
+    features_rest(N, 15, 3) # SH rest (deg 1..3)
+    (also affordance (N,1), clip_embeds (N,3) — Splat-MOVER extras, IGNORE for RGB)
+
+
+================================================================================
+ 1. WORLD -> MODEL-FRAME TRANSFORM
+================================================================================
+The renderer places a camera pose into the model (gaussian) frame with:
+    Tc2g = Tw2g @ T_c2w              # camera-to-model (NS), OpenGL/nerfstudio cam axes
+T_c2w is camera->world in the FiGS **NED** world frame.
+Tw2g input frame  = NED
+Tw2g output frame = NS (nerfstudio/model frame the gaussians live in)
+Tw2g already folds in the dataparser scale (its 3x3 block ~ 0.1261 * R).
+
+dataparser_scale = 0.12614457225472883
+
+dataparser_transform (3x4)  [from pipeline.datamanager.train_dataparser_outputs]:
+  [ 0.9999979734420776,  -1.695393439149484e-05,  0.0020061566028743982, -1.2423747777938843 ]
+  [-1.695393439149484e-05, 0.9998571872711182,    0.01690067909657955,   -0.6352672576904297 ]
+  [-0.0020061566028743982,-0.01690067909657955,   0.9998551607131958,    -1.4881443977355957 ]
+
+Tw2g (4x4)  [NED -> NS, the matrix actually used for rendering]:
+  [ 0.12614431661544656,   2.138646801849853e-06, -0.00025306576654559085, -0.15671883492487332 ]
+  [-2.138646801849853e-06,-0.1261265572041315,    -0.0021319289354524646,  -0.08013551648879384 ]
+  [-0.00025306576654559085,0.0021319289354524646, -0.12612630156484925,    -0.18772133850562778 ]
+  [ 0.0,                    0.0,                    0.0,                     1.0 ]
+
+NOTE on gaussians vs dataparser: means/quats are the RAW ckpt params, already in
+the NS model frame. The dataparser transform is NOT applied to the gaussians at
+load — ONLY the camera is transformed (via Tw2g). Verified: quats fed to the
+rasterizer == raw ckpt quats (max abs diff 0.0).
+
+
+================================================================================
+ 2. REFERENCE RENDER (drone mocap [0,0,1.5], yaw 0, forward camera)
+================================================================================
+Reference drone/camera pose:
+  drone_pos_mocap   = [0.0, 0.0, 1.5]
+  drone_quat_xyzw   = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]   (identity, yaw 0; in NED)
+  drone_pos_ned     = [0.0, 0.0, -1.5]
+
+T_c2w_ned (4x4, forward camera-to-world in NED; OpenGL/nerfstudio cam axes):
+  [ 0.0,  0.0, -1.0,  0.10 ]
+  [ 1.0,  0.0,  0.0, -0.03 ]
+  [ 0.0, -1.0,  0.0, -1.51 ]
+  [ 0.0,  0.0,  0.0,  1.0  ]
+
+THE viewmat actually passed to the rasterizer (world/NS -> camera, OpenCV axes:
+x-right, y-down, z-forward). Built by sagesplat get_viewmat():
+    R = c2w[:3,:3] * [1,-1,-1]       # OpenGL -> OpenCV/gsplat flip of y,z
+    R_inv = R.T ;  t_inv = -R_inv @ c2w[:3,3]
+    viewmat = [[R_inv, t_inv],[0,0,0,1]]
+where c2w = Tc2g = Tw2g @ T_c2w_ned.
+
+viewmat (4x4, world/NS -> cam, OpenCV):
+  [ 2.1386467778938822e-06, -0.12612655758857727,   0.0021319289226084948, -0.009229300543665886 ]
+  [-0.0002530657802708447,  -0.0021319289226084948,-0.12612630426883698,    0.0001407026284141466 ]
+  [ 0.12614431977272034,    -2.1386467778938822e-06,-0.0002530657802708447,  0.018130268901586533 ]
+  [ 0.0,                      0.0,                    0.0,                    1.0 ]
+
+K (3x3, carl_dual FORWARD pinhole intrinsics, no downscale):
+  [ 502.2632141113281,   0.0,               506.3970947265625 ]
+  [ 0.0,                 500.6736145019531, 385.4100036621094 ]
+  [ 0.0,                 0.0,               1.0 ]
+width  = 1024
+height = 768
+
+Camera convention: viewmat is WORLD->CAMERA in OpenCV axes (x-right, y-down,
+z-forward). It is NOT a c2w. gsplat-1.5.3 `viewmats` expects exactly this.
+
+rasterization kwargs used:
+  packed         = False
+  near_plane     = 0.001
+  far_plane      = 1e10
+  render_mode    = "RGB+ED"   ("RGB" is fine if you don't need depth)
+  sh_degree      = 3
+  rasterize_mode = "classic"  (=> opacity is plain sigmoid; NO antialias comp.)
+
+Background composite (needed for an EXACT pixel match):
+  background = [0.14900000393390656, 0.1647000014781952, 0.21570000052452087]
+  rgb = render[..., :3] + (1 - alpha) * background
+  rgb = clamp(rgb, 0, 1)
+
+Reference PNG: reference_forward_render.png (1024x768) — compare against this.
+
+
+================================================================================
+ 3. GAUSSIAN ACTIVATION RECIPE (apply to the RAW ckpt gauss_params.*)
+================================================================================
+Verified by measuring the exact tensors handed to the rasterizer (no crop at
+inference -> full arrays):
+  scales     = exp(scales)                       # verified: max abs diff 2.5e-6
+  opacities  = sigmoid(opacities).squeeze(-1)    # verified: max abs diff 9e-8, -> (N,)
+  quats      = RAW (unnormalized)                # verified diff 0.0; rasterization
+                                                 #   L2-normalizes internally
+  colors     = cat(features_dc[:,None,:], features_rest)  -> SH coeffs (N, 16, 3)
+               passed with sh_degree=3; gsplat does SH->RGB internally.
+  means      = RAW (already in NS model frame)
+
+(If you instead reproduce SH by hand: rgbs = SH_eval(deg=3, viewdirs, coeffs);
+ rgbs = clamp(rgbs + 0.5, min=0). The DC-only convention is SH2RGB(x)=x*C0+0.5,
+ C0 = 0.28209479177387814. But easiest is to let gsplat.rasterization do it via
+ the colors=(N,16,3) + sh_degree=3 path.)
+
+
+================================================================================
+ 4. FINAL POLICY IMAGE SPEC (render -> what the VLA server receives)
+================================================================================
+  render resolution : 1024 x 768   (the K above)
+  resize            : to image_size x image_size, square  (runs used image_size = 224)
+  interpolation     : PIL BILINEAR
+  channel order     : RGB  (no swap; RGB-era convention)
+  sent as           : uint8 (image_size, image_size, 3)
+
+
+================================================================================
+ STANDALONE gsplat-1.5.3 REPRODUCTION (drop-in)
+================================================================================
+import torch
+import numpy as np
+from gsplat import rasterization            # gsplat 1.5.3
+
+DEV = "cuda"
+ckpt = torch.load("step-000029999.ckpt", map_location=DEV)
+sd = ckpt["pipeline"]                       # nerfstudio pipeline state_dict
+
+# Gauss params. Key prefix is usually "_model.gauss_params." (newer) or
+# "_model." (older splatfacto). Print sd.keys() if unsure.
+def g(name):
+    for pre in ("_model.gauss_params.", "_model."):
+        if pre + name in sd:
+            return sd[pre + name].to(DEV)
+    raise KeyError(name)
+
+means     = g("means")                      # (N,3) RAW, NS frame
+quats     = g("quats")                      # (N,4) RAW (rasterization normalizes)
+scales    = torch.exp(g("scales"))          # (N,3)
+opacities = torch.sigmoid(g("opacities")).squeeze(-1)   # (N,)
+colors    = torch.cat([g("features_dc")[:, None, :], g("features_rest")], dim=1)  # (N,16,3)
+
+# Camera (from this handoff). Either paste the dumped viewmat directly:
+viewmat = torch.tensor([
+  [ 2.1386467778938822e-06, -0.12612655758857727,   0.0021319289226084948, -0.009229300543665886],
+  [-0.0002530657802708447,  -0.0021319289226084948,-0.12612630426883698,    0.0001407026284141466],
+  [ 0.12614431977272034,    -2.1386467778938822e-06,-0.0002530657802708447,  0.018130268901586533],
+  [ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]], device=DEV, dtype=torch.float32)[None]     # (1,4,4)
+
+# ...or compute it for an arbitrary NED camera pose T_c2w_ned:
+#   Tw2g = <paste Tw2g 4x4>
+#   c2w  = Tw2g @ T_c2w_ned
+#   R = c2w[:3,:3] * torch.tensor([1.,-1.,-1.], device=DEV)
+#   R_inv = R.T ; t_inv = -R_inv @ c2w[:3,3]
+#   viewmat = torch.eye(4); viewmat[:3,:3]=R_inv; viewmat[:3,3]=t_inv; viewmat=viewmat[None]
+
+K = torch.tensor([[502.2632141113281, 0.0, 506.3970947265625],
+                  [0.0, 500.6736145019531, 385.4100036621094],
+                  [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]], device=DEV, dtype=torch.float32)[None]   # (1,3,3)
+W, H = 1024, 768
+bg = torch.tensor([0.14900000393390656, 0.1647000014781952, 0.21570000052452087], device=DEV)
+
+render, alpha, info = rasterization(
+    means=means, quats=quats, scales=scales, opacities=opacities, colors=colors,
+    viewmats=viewmat, Ks=K, width=W, height=H,
+    packed=False, near_plane=0.001, far_plane=1e10,
+    render_mode="RGB", sh_degree=3, rasterize_mode="classic",
+)
+rgb = (render[..., :3] + (1.0 - alpha) * bg).clamp(0, 1).squeeze(0)   # (H,W,3) in [0,1]
+# uint8: (rgb*255).byte().cpu().numpy()   -> compare vs reference_forward_render.png
+
+# Policy image: PIL.Image.fromarray(rgb_uint8).resize((224,224), Image.BILINEAR), RGB.
+
